why 4 3/4 DOUBLING Alto (A–B) and tenor (C♯–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 8 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The D chord arrives on the last eighth and carries over the bar line while the bass leaps an octave. The new bar decorates the same chord before moving on.
why 8 0 RANGE The bass leaps up an octave to a high D and walks down D–C–B–A: a short high stretch that keeps it close to the tenor.
why 8 0 LEAPS The tenor rises E–A–D through chord notes as the bass leaps an octave. Leaping through the notes of the chord keeps the line clear.
why 2 0 CROSSING The alto drops to B while the tenor leaps up to E, so they trade places for a beat. The tenor's E is the seventh of the coming V⁶₅, and the alto climbs back to F♯ at once.
why 6 3/4 SPACING The new phrase starts with the alto high on B and the tenor low on F♯. The tenor climbs G♯–A♯–B over the next eighths and the gap closes.
why 7 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C♯–B) and tenor (A♯–B) both move by step onto B; the tenor's A♯ is a leading tone and must rise.
why 7 1/2 OVERLAP The tenor drops a fifth to E, below where the bass just was, while the bass falls a fourth to C♯. Both move down together, so the order stays clear.
why 10 1/4 SPACING The alto holds a high B while the tenor drops to a low F♯. The gap lasts two eighths.
why 10 3/8 SPACING The tenor is still on its low F♯ and leaps up an octave on the next beat.
